WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.760
- [Tanya] All women in the
United States are impacted

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.370
but black women are the most vulnerable

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.826
and therefore dying at higher levels.

00:00:07.826 --> 00:00:10.909
(Introduction Music)

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.100
December of 2019,

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.936
Shawnee Benton Gibson,
Shamony's mother put

00:00:22.936 --> 00:00:26.070
out essentially a call
to action on Instagram

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.300
inviting people to a
celebration of Shamony's life.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.510
Paula saw that on Instagram
and said, "Hey, I think

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.810
we should reach out and,
and try to film, film them."

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.089
Shawnee actually picked up the phone.

00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:41.490
She talked with Paula, Paula told her

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:42.810
about what we were trying to do.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.900
And she invited us in to film Aftershock.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.480
Which was the life celebration.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.510
Amber Rose Isaac passed
away in April of 2020,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:52.343
and Omari,

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:55.470
when he heard about
Amber's passing reached out

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:59.880
to Bruce McIntyre. He was there
as a support system for him

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.710
told him we were working on the film.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.870
Asked Bruce if he would be open to us,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.100
talking to him and we did.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.069
And, and that's how we
ended up with everybody.

00:01:08.069 --> 00:01:11.152
(Instrumental Music)

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.640
- [Paula] The way we started was really

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.230
following our protagonists,

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:19.710
following the stories
of Shamony and Amber,

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.970
and their families, right?

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.470
Shawnee, Omari, and Bruce.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.150
Through their stories,

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.240
we kind of compliment their
stories with information.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.580
So that provided the backbone

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.000
and the information
came secondary to that.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.380
We had a shared vision.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.780
We didn't want this to
be a talking heads film,

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:37.680
a survey film.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.227
This is a character driven film.

00:01:40.227 --> 00:01:43.290
And the way we wanted to educate for lack

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.123
of a better word,

00:01:44.123 --> 00:01:46.110
was really through the lived experiences

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.501
of our protagonists. The
human to human connection.

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:51.509
Really going into Shamony
and Amber's stories.

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:53.940
That's the best part of
filmmaking is storytelling.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.010
And that's how you get
people to pay attention.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.200
And then you slip in the statistics.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.930
- [Tanya] If you really deal
with the most vulnerable,

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.640
you deal with everyone, talking about the

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.100
maternal health crisis
in the United States.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.610
I mean, all women in the
United States are impacted

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.900
by the fact that we don't
have midwifery integrated

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.058
into women's healthcare, right?

00:02:15.058 --> 00:02:18.570
But black women are the most
vulnerable and therefore

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.040
dying at higher levels.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.790
So you tell the story of what's
happening with black women

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.420
and certainly the racism
has an impact as well.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.120
But then you also get at the
overall healthcare system

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.090
in general, that where
everybody is impacted by.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.310
We really had a shared vision
about, and that was one

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.650
of the wonderful things
about our collaboration

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.080
from the first time that we
sat down and started talking.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.263
I think we both understood how
we wanted to tell this story

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:46.890
and we did not want it to
be a doom and gloom story.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.311
We wanted to show that there are solutions

00:02:49.311 --> 00:02:53.400
to the problem dignified and
safe birthing should be first

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.000
and foremost, I think for,
for me and for Paula, you know

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.760
we, we are hoping that
there's a cultural shift

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.260
in how we think about birth, right?

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.450
I think often the first
thing that comes to

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.550
people's minds first, it's like,
oh, I'm so excited for you.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.320
Oh, that's gonna be really painful.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.153
You know?

00:03:08.153 --> 00:03:10.410
So instead of having the
conversation about the pain

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.080
of birthing all the time,
or are the negative sides.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.050
That we really want people
to come away, feeling hopeful

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:21.050
excited, inspired, you know,
moved by the film to action.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.327
- [Paula] Through the, the filming of

00:03:23.327 --> 00:03:27.270
of Felicia's birth specifically
and her birth journey.

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:30.690
We wanted to reclaim what
birth looks like in film.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.636
Usually doesn't look like that.

00:03:32.636 --> 00:03:36.360
It's, it's a, woman's
screaming and ranting and she's

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.610
in a hospital and it's like
this like horrible thing.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:42.495
And we really took our time
to show a real process.

00:03:42.495 --> 00:03:44.550
It's a long scene.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.230
And, and we did that purposely.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.170
So we wanted to really
show what it could be like

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:52.331
she's the hope and what
every woman deserves.

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:54.331
(Music)

00:03:56.694 --> 00:04:01.013
You know, we want birthing
people, families, women, to, to

00:04:01.013 --> 00:04:04.821
to know that they have choices
around birthing options.

00:04:04.821 --> 00:04:08.130
They can choose what kind of
healthcare provider they want.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.570
Maybe they want a midwife.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:10.403
You know?

00:04:10.403 --> 00:04:12.714
Maybe they want an OB there's
different birth settings that

00:04:12.714 --> 00:04:14.490
that they can choose from.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:17.070
So people don't realize that
there's other ways to do this.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.800
Every other industrialized
country has choice.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.630
And their rates are far better than ours.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.070
And we have these horrible rates

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:26.250
because they force everyone into a system.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:27.390
As Neel, Dr. Shah

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.850
says in our film, "OB's
are trained surgeons.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:31.770
Labor and delivery floors
are set up like ICUs,"

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.050
you know 99% of pregnant people.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:34.883
You put them there.

00:04:34.883 --> 00:04:35.940
You're gonna see a lot of surgery.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.470
Pregnancy is not a one size fits all.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.510
So we hope to show choice.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.460
I really hope there's accountability

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.250
within the medical system.
Hospitals, administrators

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.070
insurance companies, providers, you know

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.530
they need to provide human-centered care.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.380
I was pregnant myself
through making this film

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.150
the first part of it, and then postpartum

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.050
then it was a pandemic.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.390
My daughter was born in March, 2020.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.018
That time period was, was especially hard.

00:05:00.018 --> 00:05:02.370
I did speak to someone, you know

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.160
a doula therapist once
a week to decompress

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.840
and really try to, you know

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.860
I tried to separate the work
from, from the personal life.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.060
But as Tanya said, it was a,
a heavy time in the world.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:16.710
We're covering heavy subject matter.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.250
And I really just took
strength from the team

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.727
making the film with us

00:05:21.727 --> 00:05:24.960
from the protagonist, from
Omari, Shawnee and Bruce.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:25.793
And when I, you know

00:05:25.793 --> 00:05:29.940
shifted that mindset of
like despair, you know

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.570
and shifted it towards, you
know, this film like action and

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.060
and the impact the film can
make, then, then it like

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.370
you know, charged me up to,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:39.930
to focus on that part of this.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.240
- [Tanya] You know, I hope
people come away understanding

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.620
that the U.S maternal mortality crisis,

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:48.360
how it's affecting black women is all part

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.170
of the overall reproductive
justice conversation.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.370
You know, there really is an attack

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:56.488
on women and it's not
just abortion rights.

00:05:56.488 --> 00:06:00.060
It's also birthing rights and
birthing choices and options.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.850
So I hope people understand
that this is all part

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.300
of one conversation. And we
have to care about our women.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.920
And that's how we care about our families.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.803
And that's how we care
about our communities.

00:06:12.256 --> 00:06:14.756
(Outro Music)

